Your overall description is correct. The main correction is that the residual PSD and RMS characterize the residual; they do not generate the AR coefficients. `aryule` estimates the \(A\) coefficients and innovation variance from the residual’s autocorrelation using the Yule–Walker method. \(B\) is then the square root of that variance. [MathWorks `aryule` documentation](https://www.mathworks.com/help/signal/ref/aryule.html)

Assuming a technical engineering audience, this is a clean two-slide version.

## Slide 1 — Test data was decomposed into bias, tones, and colored noise

### On-slide content

**Objective**

Replace a constant-RMS white-noise approximation with a regime-specific test-derived model:

$$
e[k]
=
b_{\mathrm{bias}}
+
\sum_{j=1}^{N_t}A_j
\sin(2\pi f_jk/F_s+\phi_j)
+
y[k]
$$

**Identification process**

1. Select the operating regime of interest.
2. Downsample the test data to the required model rate \(F_s\).

   * No anti-alias filter was applied.
3. Estimate and remove the stationary bias.
4. Use the FFT to locate dominant tone frequencies.
5. Fit tone amplitudes and phases using least squares.
6. Subtract the tone model to obtain the stochastic residual.
7. Fit an order-\(p\) AR/all-pole model to the residual using Yule–Walker.

$$
[A,e]=\operatorname{aryule}(r,p),
\qquad B=\sqrt{e}
$$

**Model outputs**

$$
\{F_s,\ b_{\mathrm{bias}},\ f_j,\ A_j,\ \phi_j,\ A(z),\ B\}
$$

### Speaker script

> The original simulation represented sensor noise using white Gaussian noise multiplied by a measured RMS value. That reproduced the total noise magnitude, but it could not reproduce the frequency-dependent coloration or the discrete tones observed in the test data.
>
> I therefore identified a separate model for each operating regime. First, I extracted the regime of interest and downsampled the data to the rate required by the simulation. No anti-alias filter was applied, so the identified model represents the spectrum of the resulting downsampled sequence, including any aliased content.
>
> I calculated the stationary bias as the regime mean relative to the expected baseline and removed it before modeling the dynamic noise.
>
> I then used the FFT to identify the dominant tone frequencies. The number of tones was specified for each regime. Once the frequencies were selected, I performed a simultaneous sine-and-cosine least-squares fit to estimate each tone’s amplitude and phase.
>
> After subtracting the fitted tones, the remaining signal was treated as the stochastic residual. Its Welch PSD and integrated RMS were calculated to characterize its spectral shape and power.
>
> Finally, I applied MATLAB’s `aryule` function to the residual. This uses the Yule–Walker method to estimate the coefficients of an order-\(p\) autoregressive, or all-pole, model. It returns the denominator coefficients \(A\) and the estimated white-noise innovation variance. The filter numerator \(B\) is the square root of that variance.
>
> The resulting parameter set contains the bias, tone frequencies, amplitudes and phases, AR coefficients, innovation scale, and model sample rate.

The conceptual foundation is consistent with Durbin’s treatment of fitting autoregressive time-series models and Kasdin’s description of producing colored noise by driving a linear recursive filter with white noise. [Durbin (1959)](sandbox:/workspace/scratch/171de1ec3cfd/project_sources/01-ISMS_1959_244-2-.pdf) and [Kasdin (1995)](sandbox:/workspace/scratch/171de1ec3cfd/project_sources/02-Discrete_simulation_of_colored_noise_and_stochastic_processes_and_1_f_sup__spl_alpha___power_law_noise_generation-2-.pdf)

