## Slide 2 — The simulation reconstructs the identified model in real time

### On-slide content

**AR-shaped stochastic residual**

$$
y[k]
=
\frac{
B\,w[k]-\sum_{m=1}^{p}a_m y[k-m]
}{a_0},
\qquad
w[k]\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)
$$

**Simulation sequence**

1. Generate one unit-Gaussian innovation per sensor update.
2. Apply the recursive AR filter using persistent output history.
3. Generate the deterministic tones using simulation time:

$$
s[k]=\sum_j A_j\sin(2\pi f_jt_k+\phi_j)
$$

4. Combine the stochastic residual and tones:

$$
n_{\mathrm{sensor}}[k]=y[k]+s[k]
$$

5. Transform the identified sensor-axis noise into the platform frame.
6. Pass the result through the existing sensor-error and delta-measurement logic.

**Implementation requirements**

* Update the model at the same \(F_s\) used for identification.
* Store only AR residual values in the AR history.
* Do not multiply the output by the old noise RMS.
* Add the identified bias separately through the existing bias path.

### Speaker script

> The MATLAB parameters are transferred directly into the simulation. At every sensor update, the simulation generates a zero-mean, unit-standard-deviation Gaussian innovation.
>
> That innovation is scaled by \(B\) and passed through the recursive all-pole filter defined by the \(A\) coefficients. The filter history must persist between calls because the current colored-noise value depends on previous AR outputs.
>
> Separately, the deterministic tone contribution is reconstructed from the identified frequencies, amplitudes, and phases. The existing global simulation time can be used as long as it is in seconds and corresponds to the current sensor sample. If a model begins at a regime transition, the time can instead be measured relative to the beginning of that regime.
>
> The tone sum is added to the AR residual to reconstruct the complete dynamic noise model. This signal is placed on the identified sensor axis and transformed into the platform frame using the existing sensor-to-platform transformation.
>
> The transformed noise then enters the simulation’s existing delta-measurement calculation. The bias remains a separate error term, and the old RMS multiplier is removed because the AR innovation scale \(B\) already sets the stochastic output magnitude.
>
> The filter must update at the same sample rate used during identification. Otherwise, both the AR frequency response and the generated tone frequencies will be represented incorrectly.

Kasdin specifically motivates recursive AR/IIR generation as a real-time method requiring only previous output history, which aligns closely with this C++ implementation. [Kasdin (1995)](sandbox:/workspace/scratch/171de1ec3cfd/project_sources/02-Discrete_simulation_of_colored_noise_and_stochastic_processes_and_1_f_sup__spl_alpha___power_law_noise_generation-2-.pdf)

One conditional point to verify before presenting: if \(F_{s,\mathrm{in}}/F_s\) is not an integer—such as 1000/80—the rounded-index code does not create perfectly uniform 80 Hz samples. If your actual ratio is integer, the simple stride method is uniform and this concern does not apply.
